<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rafic Hariri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a>–<a href="/years/2005">2005</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rafiq al-Hariri</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rafic Hariri was a Lebanese businessman who made his fortune in Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and served as prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> in 1992–1998 and 2000–2004, leading the reconstruction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> after the civil war. His assassination by a car bomb on 14 February 2005 set off mass protests, the Cedar Revolution, which forced Syrian troops to leave Lebanon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rafic-hariri.jpg" alt="Rafic Hariri" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: European Commission - Christian Lambiotte · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Visit_of_Rafic_Hariri,_Lebanese_Prime_Minister,_to_the_EC_P010848002H_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rafic Bahaa El Deen al-Hariri was born on 1 November 1944 to a modest Sunni Muslim family in the port city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>. He went to school there and graduated in business administration from Beirut Arab University. In 1965 he went to Saudi Arabia, where he taught for a short time before moving into the construction industry, and in 1978 he took Saudi citizenship alongside his Lebanese one.</p><p>After a small subcontracting firm failed, he went into business with the French construction firm Oger on a hotel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a>, whose timely completion won praise from King Khaled. Hariri took over Oger, formed Saudi Oger, and it became the main builder for the Saudi royal family's major projects. In 1979 he founded the Islamic Association for Culture and Education, later the Hariri Foundation, which built educational facilities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. During the civil war he acted as an envoy of the Saudi royal family and helped to prepare the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif-agreement">Taif Agreement</a>, which Saudi Arabia organised to bring the warring factions together.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>In 1992 Hariri became the first prime minister after the civil war, under President Elias Hrawi, and also served as finance minister. He headed the reconstruction plan Horizon 2000, whose centrepiece was Solidere, a company formed to rebuild central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. Solidere was given powers of compulsory purchase and compensated owners in company shares rather than cash; critics said owners were underpaid. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">Inflation</a>, running at 131 percent in 1992, fell to 12 percent within two years, but public debt rose sharply. His first term ended in 1998, after a power struggle with President Émile Lahoud.</p><p>He was appointed again in October 2000. In his second term the Israeli withdrawal from southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> ended an occupation of eighteen years. In September 2004 he defended UN Security Council Resolution 1559, which called for all remaining foreign forces to withdraw from Lebanon, and on 20 October 2004 he resigned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 14 February 2005 explosives equivalent to about 1,800 kilograms of TNT, hidden in a parked van, were detonated as Hariri's motorcade passed the St. George Hotel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. Twenty-three people died, among them Hariri, several bodyguards and the former economy minister Bassel Fleihan. On 14 March between 1.2 and 1.5 million people assembled in Beirut to demand an end to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s occupation. These protests, called the Cedar Revolution, were followed by the withdrawal of Syrian troops.</p><p>The Special Tribunal for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> was created at his instigation. In August 2020 it convicted Salim Ayyash, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hezbollah</a> member, in absentia and acquitted three others; the judges found no evidence of involvement by Hezbollah's leadership. Beirut International Airport was renamed after Hariri in 2005, and his son Saad Hariri succeeded him as leader of the Future Movement.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rafic Hariri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in Beirut, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=1983" aria-label="Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5453 946 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rafic Hariri"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5903 1216h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5903 1216h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=1983">What was here in 1983? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
